Uterine Fibroids (Leiomyomas)
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Menorrhagia, Pelvic pressure/heaviness, Firm irregularly enlarged uterus

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-047
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe pelvic pain and acute urinary retention secondary to a large pelvic mass.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of excruciating pelvic pain and a total inability to void urine for the last 8 hours in a patient with a known history of heavy menses. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to decompress the bladder via catheterization, rule out fibroid torsion or degeneration, and provide immediate parenteral analgesia to prevent emergency department transfer before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have this massive, hard pressure in my stomach, the pain is unbearable, and I have not been able to pee all day.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a two-year history of progressively heavy menstrual bleeding (menorrhagia) and prolonged menses. She complains of chronic symptoms of profound iron deficiency anemia, such as severe fatigue and pallor, due to chronic blood loss. Over the last month, she has felt significant pelvic pressure and a sense of heaviness in the lower abdomen. Today, this pressure escalated into sharp, acute pelvic pain, accompanied by an inability to pass urine (acute urinary retention) due to anterior compression of the bladder. She also reports recent severe constipation resulting from posterior compression.
Functional Status: Bedbound, actively writhing in pain, unable to find a comfortable position.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of acute renal injury due to retention.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Patient is pale, diaphoretic, and clutching her lower abdomen in severe distress.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals a firm, irregularly enlarged, nodular uterus palpable midway to the umbilicus, consistent with a 14-week gestation. There is exquisite focal tenderness over the right superior aspect of the mass (highly suspicious for acute red degeneration of a fibroid). Below this mass, there is a dull, tense, palpable bladder indicating severe distension. No rebound peritonism.
Environmental Observations: Clear path to the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Took maximum doses of ibuprofen and paracetamol with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Uterine Fibroids (Leiomyomas) with acute red degeneration and secondary urinary retention - Status: Acute mechanical complication
Differentials:

Ovarian Torsion - Unlikely given the physical exam revealing a firmly enlarged, central, irregular uterine mass rather than a discrete, unilateral, mobile adnexal mass.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out by the absence of right lower quadrant peritonism, lack of fever, and the clear presence of a massive, firm pelvic mass causing mechanical obstruction.

Ectopic Pregnancy - Definitively ruled out by a negative point-of-care urine beta-hCG test.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to retention)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute mechanical and vascular crisis driven by large uterine fibroids. The diagnostic tetrad of menorrhagia, pelvic pressure, and a firm irregularly enlarged uterus is clinically confirmed. The acute presentation is dual-fold: sharp, severe pain indicative of fibroid degeneration (outgrowing its blood supply) and total urinary retention due to the physical mass compressing the urethra. Urgent after-hours intervention was absolutely mandatory to mechanically decompress the bladder via catheterization to prevent renal injury, and to administer potent intramuscular analgesia for the severe ischemic pain of the degenerating fibroid, preventing a highly distressed emergency hospital admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed sterile in-and-out catheterization yielding 700mL of clear urine, instantly relieving the suprapubic distension pressure. Administered intramuscular ketorolac 30mg for the severe inflammatory pain of the degenerating fibroid. Prescribed oral tramadol 50mg every 6 hours as needed. Generated an urgent referral for a formal transabdominal/transvaginal ultrasound and a fast-tracked gynecology clinic appointment for surgical planning.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain regular bladder emptying while the mass is inflamed, attempt to void exactly every two hours, leaning completely forward to physically shift the fibroid off the bladder neck; achieving successful, low-pressure emptying will reinforce the habit of timed, positional voiding. To manage the acute pain flare, continuously apply a high-heat wrap to the lower abdomen; feeling the muscular spasms relax will encourage reliance on localized physical therapy over constant strong medication.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heating pad recommended.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review tomorrow morning to arrange the expedited formal pelvic ultrasound and gynecological surgical referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department if you become completely unable to pass urine again for more than 6 hours, if the pain returns to an unbearable level despite the tramadol, or if you develop a high fever (over 38.5 C).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The acute pain is likely from the fibroid losing its blood supply, but formal ultrasound imaging is required tomorrow to confirm this and plan treatment.
Clinical Course: The injection will provide profound pain relief for several hours, and emptying the bladder has removed the mechanical pressure. The oral medications must be taken strictly to maintain this relief.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you cannot pass urine again, or if you experience sudden, catastrophic pelvic pain.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have a large fibroid tumor on your uterus. It has likely grown so fast that it outgrew its own blood supply, causing severe inflammation and pain (called red degeneration). Because of its large size, it was also physically crushing your bladder shut. The injection blocks the severe nerve pain, and draining your bladder manually has removed the immediate blockage. You need an urgent scan tomorrow to plan for its removal.
Actionable Steps: Take the tramadol at the earliest sign of the pain returning. Attempt to empty your bladder completely every two hours using the leaning forward technique, and see your regular GP first thing tomorrow.

